4. Credits for ideas and research
---------------------------------

Mickey & Donald Collection - MIT
  https://github.com/Darhkfox/MickeyAndDonaldCollection, copyright (c) 2026
  Darhkfox. Its reverse-engineering notes (docs/re_qs_wide.md,
  docs/re_woi_wide.md) gave the RAM and routine addresses used by this
  project's QS and WOI widescreen hooks, checked here
  against the games; its launcher's live game previews inspired the
  launchers' attract clips. No code was copied.

Sonic community disassemblies (Sonic Retro and contributors)
  The names and meanings of SH1, 2, 3 and SHK RAM variables and routines
  used in comments and hooks (Game_paused, camera and level layout variables,
  object fields) follow the community's disassembly conventions.

Coverage measurement (engine/tools/kpi.py, KPI "reference coverage")
  On the owner's machine, kpi.py reads lists of code addresses published by
  other Genesis recompilation and reverse-engineering projects (the
  segagenesisrecomp family's disassembly label and function lists, the Mickey &
  Donald Collection notes) to count how many of the routines they know as code
  our generated C covers. Only those counts are committed (docs/kpi/); the lists
  are read in place, never copied here, and never fed to the recompiler.
  docs/kpi/references.json holds their local paths only.

Not used: segagenesisrecomp (PolyForm Noncommercial) - no code from it is in
this repository.


5. SHK1 (games/shk1)
--------------------------------------

Our own combination, not a Sega product. Its ROM image is built on the
player's machine from their SH1 (REV00) and SHK2 (SHK + SH2) dumps (tools/make_image.py); the repository holds only our
own 68000 code (k1.s, k1.srec), addresses and mappings. The built image and the
port made from it fall under section 1: keep them to yourself.
